 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000001", "t": "The Signature Sober Root", "g": "Foundations", "name": "The Signature Sober Root", "title": "The Signature Sober Root", "description": "The Signature Sober Root is the JAH answer to the drunken stance: a stance calibration method that builds the low base from measurements instead of mimicry. The student measures the shoulder width, marks one and a half widths on the floor with tape, and builds the staggered stance to the marks, checking knee bend with a simple plumb line - a string with a weight - dropped from the front knee. When the weight hangs over the toes, the bend is right. This Signature-generated original instruction treats the stance as an engineered structure: wide base, low mass, hidden weight line. Three thirty-second holds per side per session, logged with the measured knee position, replace guesswork with repeatability.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: measure the shoulder width and mark one and a half widths on the floor with tape. Step 2: build the staggered stance to the marks, front foot on the front mark. Step 3: hang the plumb line from the front knee; adjust the bend until the weight hangs over the toes. Step 4: set the torso list by holding a book lightly on the head - five degrees of list keeps the book steady. Step 5: hold thirty seconds per side, three sets, logging the knee position each session. Step 6: practice the weight shift drill: move the list from side to side without lifting the feet, ten shifts. Step 7: remove the tape after two weeks and build the stance from memory, then check against the marks. The common errors are eyeballing the width instead of measuring, skipping the log, which hides drift, and removing the tape before the body has memorized the position.", "key_points": ["The stance is built to floor marks at one and a half shoulder widths - measured, not guessed.", "A plumb line from the front knee verifies the bend: the weight must hang over the toes.", "A book balanced lightly on the head calibrates the five-degree torso list.", "Three thirty-second holds per side per session, logged with knee positions.", "The tape comes off after two weeks; memory is then checked against the marks."], "requirements": ["Tape for floor marks, a string with a weight for the plumb line, a light book.", "A training log for the knee positions.", "Clear floor space for the marked stance."], "standards": ["The stance built to the marks holds thirty seconds per side for three sets.", "The plumb line hangs over the toes on every check.", "The tapeless stance matches the marks within one inch on the check."], "citations": ["Smith R W. Chinese Boxing: Masters and Methods. North Atlantic Books; 1974.", "Wong Kiew Kit. The Art of Shaolin Kung Fu. Tuttle Publishing; 2002."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 1, "ladder_tier": "foundations", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"The Signature Sober Root - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000001 [Foundations]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # stance calibration: marks, plumb check, hold sets\n    shoulder_width_in = 18.0\n    mark_span_in = 1.5 * shoulder_width_in\n    plumb_target = \"over the toes\"\n    hold_s = 30.0\n    sets = 3\n    sides = 2\n    print(\"mark span: %.1f in\" % mark_span_in)\n    print(\"plumb target: %s\" % plumb_target)\n    print(\"holds: %.0f s x %d sets x %d sides\" % (hold_s, sets, sides))\n    total = hold_s * sets * sides\n    print(\"total hold time: %.0f s\" % total)\n    assert mark_span_in == 27.0\n    assert total == 180.0\n    print(\"rule: measured, not guessed - repeatability is the root\")\n    print(\"OK: Signature sober root calibrated\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000002", "t": "The Signature Monkey Seat", "g": "Foundations", "name": "The Signature Monkey Seat", "title": "The Signature Monkey Seat", "description": "The Signature Monkey Seat rebuilds the deep crouch as a timed sitting practice with intent. The student sinks into the crouch holding a light staff across the thighs - the staff must stay level, which forces the back upright and the weight even. The Signature method adds the intent ladder: thirty seconds of silent sitting, then thirty seconds naming the directions of imagined attacks, then thirty seconds of small bounces, each phase logged. This Signature-generated original instruction turns the crouch from a shape into a station: a low post from which the monkey watches, decides, and springs. Two minutes total per session, built over four weeks.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: sink into the deep crouch with a light staff resting across the thighs. Step 2: level the staff - adjust until it sits horizontal, which squares the posture. Step 3: phase one: sit silent for thirty seconds, breathing slowly, staff level. Step 4: phase two: for thirty seconds, name imagined attack directions - left, high, low - turning only the head. Step 5: phase three: thirty seconds of small two-inch bounces, staff staying level. Step 6: rest one minute and repeat for a second round. Step 7: log the phases completed and any staff tilt noticed. The common errors are letting the staff tilt unnoticed, bouncing with the back instead of the legs, and skipping the naming phase, which is the awareness training.", "key_points": ["A light staff across the thighs enforces the level, upright crouch - tilt is instantly visible.", "Three thirty-second phases: silent sitting, direction naming, small bounces.", "The naming phase trains awareness: the head turns to imagined attacks while low.", "Two rounds per session with a one-minute rest, all phases logged.", "The crouch becomes a station: watch, decide, spring."], "requirements": ["A light staff or broom handle for the level check.", "A timer for the thirty-second phases.", "A training log."], "standards": ["Two full rounds are completed with the staff level throughout.", "All imagined directions are named without losing the crouch.", "Four weeks of sessions reach the two-minute total comfortably."], "citations": ["Chow D and Spangler R. Kung Fu: History, Philosophy, and Technique. Unique Publications; 1982.", "Smith R W. Chinese Boxing: Masters and Methods. North Atlantic Books; 1974."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 2, "ladder_tier": "foundations", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"The Signature Monkey Seat - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000002 [Foundations]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # seat practice: phases, rounds, build-up\n    phases = [(\"silent sitting\", 30), (\"direction naming\", 30), (\"bounces\", 30)]\n    rounds = 2\n    rest_s = 60\n    weeks = 4\n    print(\"phases per round:\")\n    for name, secs in phases:\n        print(\"  %-16s %d s\" % (name, secs))\n    round_s = sum(s for _, s in phases)\n    print(\"round: %d s, rounds: %d, rest: %d s\" % (round_s, rounds, rest_s))\n    total = round_s * rounds\n    print(\"total seat time: %d s over %d weeks\" % (total, weeks))\n    assert total == 180\n    assert len(phases) == 3\n    print(\"rule: the crouch is a station, not a shape\")\n    print(\"OK: Signature monkey seat held with intent\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000003", "t": "Signature Paw: The Five-Finger Seal", "g": "Foundations", "name": "Signature Paw: The Five-Finger Seal", "title": "Signature Paw: The Five-Finger Seal", "description": "The Signature Five-Finger Seal is the JAH hand-formation method: each finger gets a job, and the paw is built finger by finger instead of all at once. The index and middle fingers form the hooking pair, the ring and little fingers form the bracing pair, and the thumb is the seal that rests lightly across the bracing pair to lock the shape without tension. Students build the seal in order - hooking pair, bracing pair, thumb seal - holding each stage for ten seconds, until the full paw forms in under one second on command. This Signature-generated original instruction makes the paw a deliberate mechanism: five fingers, three jobs, one seal.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: hold the hand up and curl only the index and middle fingers into the hooking pair, leaving the rest open. Step 2: hold ten seconds, feeling the hook shape. Step 3: curl the ring and little fingers into the bracing pair behind the hook. Step 4: hold ten seconds, feeling the two pairs work together. Step 5: rest the thumb lightly across the bracing pair as the seal - light, never pressing. Step 6: hold the full seal ten seconds, then open flat. Step 7: repeat the build ten times, then practice forming the full seal on the command seal, aiming for under one second. The common errors are pressing the thumb hard, which creates tension, building the pairs out of order, and rushing the ten-second holds that teach the shape.", "key_points": ["The hand is built in three jobs: hooking pair, bracing pair, thumb seal.", "Each stage holds ten seconds so the shape is learned deliberately.", "The thumb seal rests lightly - pressure creates the tension the paw must avoid.", "Ten full builds per session, then on-command seals under one second.", "Five fingers, three jobs, one seal: the paw as a deliberate mechanism."], "requirements": ["No equipment; a mirror helps verify the finger jobs at first.", "A timer or counted seconds for the holds and the one-second target."], "standards": ["Ten staged builds are completed with the correct finger jobs each time.", "The full seal forms in under one second on the command seal.", "The forearm stays soft through all ten builds."], "citations": ["Wong Kiew Kit. The Art of Shaolin Kung Fu. Tuttle Publishing; 2002.", "Chow D and Spangler R. Kung Fu: History, Philosophy, and Technique. Unique Publications; 1982."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 3, "ladder_tier": "foundations", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Signature Paw: The Five-Finger Seal - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000003 [Foundations]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # seal build: stages, holds, builds, command target\n    stages = [\"hooking pair\", \"bracing pair\", \"thumb seal\"]\n    hold_s = 10\n    builds = 10\n    command_target_s = 1.0\n    print(\"stages: %s\" % \" -> \".join(stages))\n    print(\"hold per stage: %d s\" % hold_s)\n    print(\"staged builds: %d\" % builds)\n    print(\"command target: under %.1f s\" % command_target_s)\n    total_hold = hold_s * len(stages) * builds\n    print(\"total hold time: %d s\" % total_hold)\n    assert total_hold == 300\n    assert len(stages) == 3\n    print(\"rule: five fingers, three jobs, one seal\")\n    print(\"OK: Signature paw seals on command\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000004", "t": "The Signature Pendulum Sway", "g": "Foundations", "name": "The Signature Pendulum Sway", "title": "The Signature Pendulum Sway", "description": "The Signature Pendulum Sway turns the drunken sway into a counted instrument. The student sways with a metronome - starting at forty beats per minute, one sway per four beats - while a partner watches the head with a ruler held vertical beside it: the head must not travel more than one inch side to side. This Signature-generated original instruction replaces feeling with measurement: the sway is a pendulum with a counted period and a bounded head. As the weeks pass the metronome speeds to sixty beats per minute and the freeze calls are added, but the one-inch head rule never changes.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: set the metronome to forty beats per minute and settle into the stance. Step 2: sway one full cycle per four beats - two beats out, two beats back. Step 3: have the partner hold a ruler vertical beside the head; the head must stay within one inch of travel. Step 4: complete five minutes at forty beats per minute. Step 5: raise the metronome to fifty, then sixty beats per minute over the coming weeks. Step 6: add freeze calls at random beats; freeze with the head inside the one-inch bound. Step 7: log the metronome speed and any head-bound breaks each session. The common errors are chasing the metronome with the head instead of the hips, letting the bound slip as speed rises, and skipping the log, which hides the drift.", "key_points": ["The sway runs on a metronome: forty beats per minute rising to sixty over weeks.", "The head is bounded to one inch of travel, checked with a vertical ruler.", "One full sway cycle per four beats keeps the pendulum period honest.", "Freeze calls at random beats test the bound under surprise.", "Every session logs metronome speed and bound breaks."], "requirements": ["A metronome or metronome app.", "A partner with a ruler for the head-bound check.", "A training log."], "standards": ["Five minutes at forty beats per minute are completed inside the one-inch bound.", "Sixty beats per minute are reached with the bound still holding.", "Five freeze calls are answered without breaking the bound."], "citations": ["Smith R W. Chinese Boxing: Masters and Methods. North Atlantic Books; 1974.", "Reid H and Croucher M. The Way of the Warrior: The Paradox of the Martial Arts. Overlook Press; 1983."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 4, "ladder_tier": "foundations", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"The Signature Pendulum Sway - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000004 [Foundations]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # pendulum sway: metronome ladder, head bound, session length\n    bpm_ladder = [40, 50, 60]\n    beats_per_cycle = 4\n    head_bound_in = 1.0\n    session_min = 5\n    print(\"metronome ladder: %s bpm\" % bpm_ladder)\n    print(\"beats per sway cycle: %d\" % beats_per_cycle)\n    print(\"head bound: %.0f in\" % head_bound_in)\n    print(\"session: %d min\" % session_min)\n    cycles_at_40 = (session_min * 60 / (60.0 / 40)) / beats_per_cycle\n    print(\"sway cycles at 40 bpm: %.0f\" % cycles_at_40)\n    assert cycles_at_40 == 50.0\n    assert bpm_ladder == sorted(bpm_ladder)\n    print(\"rule: the sway is a pendulum with a counted period\")\n    print(\"OK: Signature pendulum sways inside the bound\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000005", "t": "Signature Stumble Counting", "g": "Foundations", "name": "Signature Stumble Counting", "title": "Signature Stumble Counting", "description": "Signature Stumble Counting is the JAH footwork method: every step of the drunken walk is counted, measured, and mapped. The student walks a ten-pace line marked with numbered tape, calling each step number aloud while a partner records the weave - the inches off line per step, measured with a tape. This Signature-generated original instruction makes the invisible visible: the walk that looks random is revealed as a controlled weave with a measured amplitude, and the student learns to widen or narrow the weave on command. Ten paces forward, ten back, weave logged, amplitude adjusted.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: mark a ten-pace line with numbered tape marks, one per pace. Step 2: walk the line in the drunken walk, calling each step number aloud. Step 3: the partner measures the weave at each mark - inches off the line - and logs it. Step 4: walk the line backward the same way, calling and logging. Step 5: review the log: the weave should be present at every step but the total drift near zero. Step 6: practice on command: walk the line with double weave, then half weave, proving control. Step 7: add the clap direction changes, logging the recovery steps after each clap. The common errors are calling the numbers without feeling the steps, letting total drift grow while chasing weave, and skipping the backward walk.", "key_points": ["A numbered ten-pace line turns the walk into measured data.", "Each step number is called aloud while the partner logs the weave in inches.", "The weave is present at every step while total drift stays near zero.", "Double-weave and half-weave walks on command prove the weave is controlled.", "Clap direction changes are logged with their recovery steps."], "requirements": ["Numbered tape marks for the ten-pace line and a measuring tape.", "A partner to log the weave.", "A training log for the weave data."], "standards": ["Ten paces forward and back are walked with every weave logged.", "Total drift stays under two inches for the full line.", "Double and half weave are both performed on command."], "citations": ["Chow D and Spangler R. Kung Fu: History, Philosophy, and Technique. Unique Publications; 1982.", "Reid H and Croucher M. The Way of the Warrior: The Paradox of the Martial Arts. Overlook Press; 1983."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 5, "ladder_tier": "foundations", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Signature Stumble Counting - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000005 [Foundations]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # stumble counting: paces, weave log, drift limit, amplitude control\n    paces = 10\n    weave_target_in = 4.0\n    drift_limit_in = 2.0\n    amplitudes = [\"half weave\", \"normal weave\", \"double weave\"]\n    print(\"paces: %d forward, %d back\" % (paces, paces))\n    print(\"weave target: %.0f in per step\" % weave_target_in)\n    print(\"drift limit: %.0f in total\" % drift_limit_in)\n    print(\"amplitudes on command: %s\" % \", \".join(amplitudes))\n    logged_steps = paces * 2\n    print(\"logged steps: %d\" % logged_steps)\n    assert logged_steps == 20\n    assert drift_limit_in < weave_target_in\n    assert len(amplitudes) == 3\n    print(\"rule: the walk that looks random is measured\")\n    print(\"OK: Signature stumble counted and controlled\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000006", "t": "The Signature Opening Bow", "g": "Foundations", "name": "The Signature Opening Bow", "title": "The Signature Opening Bow", "description": "The Signature Opening Bow standardizes the drunken salute into a timed ceremony with three checkpoints. The student performs the sway-bow on a six-count - sway on one, paws join on two, bow on three and four, hold on five, rise on six - while a partner checks the three checkpoints: the sway reads as genuine, the paws meet at the same chest point, and the bow holds a full breath. This Signature-generated original instruction treats ceremony as a measurable skill: the same six counts, the same checkpoints, every session, until the ceremony is identical every time. Identical ceremony, the teaching says, makes identical readiness.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: stand ready and begin the six-count aloud or in the head. Step 2: count one: take the small drunken sway. Step 3: count two: join the paws at the chest point. Step 4: counts three and four: bow the head fifteen degrees with the torso list. Step 5: count five: hold the bow for the full breath. Step 6: count six: rise and settle into the stance. Step 7: the partner checks the three checkpoints: genuine sway, same chest point, full-breath hold. Step 8: repeat three times; all three must pass all checkpoints. The common errors are rushing the counts, joining the paws at a different point each time, and cutting the hold short.", "key_points": ["The salute runs on a six-count: sway, join, bow, bow, hold, rise.", "Three checkpoints: genuine sway, same chest point, full-breath hold.", "A partner verifies the checkpoints on every ceremony.", "Three consecutive ceremonies must pass all checkpoints.", "Identical ceremony makes identical readiness."], "requirements": ["A partner to check the three checkpoints.", "The monkey paw and stance already learned."], "standards": ["Three consecutive six-count salutes pass all three checkpoints.", "The paws meet at the same chest point within one inch every time.", "The hold lasts a full breath on every rep."], "citations": ["Shahar M. The Shaolin Monastery: History, Religion, and the Chinese Martial Arts. University of Hawaii Press; 2008.", "Wong Kiew Kit. The Art of Shaolin Kung Fu. Tuttle Publishing; 2002."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 6, "ladder_tier": "foundations", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"The Signature Opening Bow - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000006 [Foundations]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # opening bow: six counts, three checkpoints, three passes\n    counts = [\"sway\", \"join paws\", \"bow\", \"bow\", \"hold\", \"rise\"]\n    checkpoints = [\"genuine sway\", \"same chest point\", \"full-breath hold\"]\n    consecutive_passes = 3\n    print(\"counts: %s\" % \" -> \".join(counts))\n    print(\"checkpoints: %s\" % \", \".join(checkpoints))\n    print(\"consecutive passes needed: %d\" % consecutive_passes)\n    total_checks = consecutive_passes * len(checkpoints)\n    print(\"total checkpoint checks: %d\" % total_checks)\n    assert len(counts) == 6\n    assert total_checks == 9\n    print(\"rule: identical ceremony makes identical readiness\")\n    print(\"OK: Signature opening bow standardized\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000007", "t": "Signature Joint Oiling", "g": "Foundations", "name": "Signature Joint Oiling", "title": "Signature Joint Oiling", "description": "Signature Joint Oiling is the JAH warm-up protocol: a fixed sequence with counted reps and a readiness checklist. The student performs the wrist circles, wrist stretches, ankle circles, toe raises, and calf drops in the same order every session, then runs the readiness checklist - wrists warm, ankles loose, no sharp pain anywhere - and only then begins falling practice. This Signature-generated original instruction removes the decision from the warm-up: the sequence never changes, the counts never change, and the checklist gates the session. A skipped warm-up, the protocol states, is a session not started.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: perform the fixed sequence in order: wrist circles, wrist stretches, ankle circles, toe raises, calf drops. Step 2: use the fixed counts: ten circles each direction, five-second stretches, fifteen raises, ten drops. Step 3: run the readiness checklist aloud: wrists warm, ankles loose, no sharp pain. Step 4: if any checklist item fails, repeat the relevant section once more. Step 5: if sharp pain appears, the session does not start - the joint rests. Step 6: log the warm-up completion with the checklist result. Step 7: begin falling practice only after a passed checklist. The common errors are changing the order, which breaks the habit, rushing the counts, and starting the session on a failed checklist.", "key_points": ["A fixed sequence in a fixed order with fixed counts - the warm-up never varies.", "The readiness checklist gates the session: warm wrists, loose ankles, no sharp pain.", "A failed checklist item means repeating the section, not skipping ahead.", "Sharp pain cancels the session; the joint rests that day.", "Completion and checklist result are logged every session."], "requirements": ["About three minutes and the discipline to keep the order fixed.", "A training log for warm-up completion."], "standards": ["The full sequence is completed in order with the fixed counts.", "The checklist passes before every falling session for one week.", "No session starts on a failed checklist."], "citations": ["Reid H and Croucher M. The Way of the Warrior: The Paradox of the Martial Arts. Overlook Press; 1983.", "Smith R W. Chinese Boxing: Masters and Methods. North Atlantic Books; 1974."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 7, "ladder_tier": "foundations", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Signature Joint Oiling - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000007 [Foundations]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # joint oiling: fixed sequence, fixed counts, checklist gate\n    sequence = [(\"wrist circles\", 40), (\"wrist stretches\", 4), (\"ankle circles\", 40),\n                (\"toe raises\", 15), (\"calf drops\", 10)]\n    checklist = [\"wrists warm\", \"ankles loose\", \"no sharp pain\"]\n    print(\"sequence:\")\n    for name, count in sequence:\n        print(\"  %-16s %d\" % (name, count))\n    print(\"checklist: %s\" % \", \".join(checklist))\n    total = sum(c for _, c in sequence)\n    print(\"total actions: %d\" % total)\n    assert total == 109\n    assert len(checklist) == 3\n    print(\"rule: a skipped warm-up is a session not started\")\n    print(\"OK: joints oiled and checklist passed\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000008", "t": "The Signature Rise", "g": "Foundations", "name": "The Signature Rise", "title": "The Signature Rise", "description": "The Signature Rise rebuilds the safe get-up as a five-phase timed drill with a guard audit. The student performs the roll-to-side, post, knee-under, leg-drive, stance sequence against a three-second timer while a partner audits the guard: one paw up at every phase, verified by a tap test - the partner taps the student shoulder mid-rise, and the guard paw must already be between the tap and the face. This Signature-generated original instruction makes the rise a verified skill: timed under three seconds and guard-audited on every rep. Twenty rises per session, ten per side, all audited.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: lie on the back and begin the five-phase rise on the timer start. Step 2: roll to the side, post the hand, bring the knee under, drive with the legs, settle into stance. Step 3: stop the timer at the stance; the target is under three seconds. Step 4: the partner performs the tap test mid-rise on random reps: a tap toward the face that the guard paw must already be blocking. Step 5: repeat for ten rises per side, the partner auditing guard on every rep. Step 6: log the times and the tap-test results. Step 7: any rep over three seconds or with a failed tap test is repeated. The common errors are sacrificing the guard for speed, which fails the audit, and practicing without the timer, which hides slowness.", "key_points": ["The five-phase rise is timed: under three seconds from back to stance.", "The guard is audited by tap test - the paw must already block the incoming tap.", "Ten rises per side per session, every rep timed and audited.", "Failed reps - over time or failed audit - are repeated, not counted.", "Times and audit results are logged every session."], "requirements": ["A mat, a timer, and a partner for the tap-test audit.", "The basic safe rise already learned."], "standards": ["Twenty rises are completed under three seconds each.", "The tap test is passed on eight of ten audited reps.", "The log shows improving times over two weeks."], "citations": ["Smith R W. Chinese Boxing: Masters and Methods. North Atlantic Books; 1974.", "Reid H and Croucher M. The Way of the Warrior: The Paradox of the Martial Arts. Overlook Press; 1983."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 8, "ladder_tier": "foundations", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"The Signature Rise - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000008 [Foundations]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # signature rise: phases, timer, audit\n    phases = [\"roll to side\", \"post hand\", \"knee under\", \"leg drive\", \"stance\"]\n    target_s = 3.0\n    rises_per_side = 10\n    audit_passes_needed = 8\n    audits = 10\n    print(\"phases: %s\" % \" -> \".join(phases))\n    print(\"target: under %.1f s\" % target_s)\n    print(\"rises per side: %d\" % rises_per_side)\n    print(\"tap audit: %d/%d passes needed\" % (audit_passes_needed, audits))\n    total = rises_per_side * 2\n    print(\"total rises: %d\" % total)\n    assert total == 20\n    assert audit_passes_needed / audits == 0.8\n    print(\"rule: timed and audited - speed never outruns guard\")\n    print(\"OK: Signature rise verified\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000009", "t": "Signature Tuck Geometry", "g": "Falling and Rolling", "name": "Signature Tuck Geometry", "title": "Signature Tuck Geometry", "description": "Signature Tuck Geometry is the JAH forward-roll method: the roll is taught as three angles that must all be correct. Angle one: the chin tucks forty-five degrees to the chest. Angle two: the rolling shoulder meets the mat at forty-five degrees to the direction of travel, setting the diagonal line. Angle three: the body curls so the knees pass within six inches of the chest. Students check each angle with a partner and a protractor card before rolling, then roll, then re-check. This Signature-generated original instruction replaces feel with geometry: three angles, all verified, then the roll. Twenty rolls per session, angles checked on the first five.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: kneel on the mat and set angle one: tuck the chin forty-five degrees, checked with the protractor card. Step 2: set angle two: place the rolling shoulder forty-five degrees to the travel direction. Step 3: set angle three: curl until the knees sit within six inches of the chest. Step 4: roll along the diagonal, holding all three angles. Step 5: rise to the stance and re-check the angles. Step 6: drill twenty rolls, checking angles on the first five of each session. Step 7: progress from kneeling to crouch to standing, re-verifying angles at each height. The common errors are setting the angles but losing them mid-roll, skipping the re-check, and progressing heights before the angles hold.", "key_points": ["Three angles define the roll: forty-five degree chin tuck, forty-five degree shoulder line, knees within six inches of the chest.", "Angles are set and verified with a protractor card before the roll.", "The first five rolls of each session are angle-checked; the rest run on the pattern.", "Heights progress kneeling to crouch to standing with angles re-verified each step.", "Twenty rolls per session keep the geometry in the body."], "requirements": ["A mat and a protractor card or printed angle guide.", "A partner to verify the angles.", "The basic forward roll already learned."], "standards": ["All three angles verify correctly before the first roll.", "Twenty rolls are completed with the angles holding on the checked reps.", "The standing roll keeps all three angles."], "citations": ["Smith R W. Chinese Boxing: Masters and Methods. North Atlantic Books; 1974.", "Reid H and Croucher M. The Way of the Warrior: The Paradox of the Martial Arts. Overlook Press; 1983."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 9, "ladder_tier": "compounded", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Signature Tuck Geometry - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000009 [Falling and Rolling]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # tuck geometry: three angles, checks, rolls\n    angles = {\"chin tuck\": 45.0, \"shoulder line\": 45.0, \"knee to chest\": 6.0}\n    checked_rolls = 5\n    total_rolls = 20\n    heights = [\"kneeling\", \"crouch\", \"standing\"]\n    print(\"angles:\")\n    for k, v in angles.items():\n        print(\"  %-14s %.0f\" % (k, v))\n    print(\"checked rolls: %d of %d\" % (checked_rolls, total_rolls))\n    print(\"heights: %s\" % \", \".join(heights))\n    assert len(angles) == 3\n    assert checked_rolls <= total_rolls\n    assert len(heights) == 3\n    print(\"rule: three angles, all verified, then the roll\")\n    print(\"OK: Signature tuck geometry holds\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000010", "t": "The Signature Back Roll Line", "g": "Falling and Rolling", "name": "The Signature Back Roll Line", "title": "The Signature Back Roll Line", "description": "The Signature Back Roll Line teaches the backward roll as a straight-line skill: the student rolls along a tape line on the mat, and the roll is only counted if it starts and ends on the line. The line exposes the two classic errors - rolling crooked over the middle of the back, and pushing off sideways with the hands - because either one leaves the line. This Signature-generated original instruction adds the hand-push audit: the hands may assist the roll but must land back on the line, palms down, beside the ears. Ten line rolls per session, counted only when the line is kept.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: lay a straight tape line on the mat and squat astride it in the crouch. Step 2: tuck the chin and roll backward along the line, over the shoulder. Step 3: land the hands back on the line, palms down, beside the ears. Step 4: finish in the crouch astride the line, facing the original direction. Step 5: count only the rolls that never leave the line; crooked rolls are repeated. Step 6: drill ten counted rolls per session. Step 7: progress from crouch to standing starts, keeping the line rule. The common errors are counting crooked rolls, which teaches crooked rolling, and letting the hands land off the line, which hides the sideways push.", "key_points": ["A tape line on the mat defines the roll path; only on-line rolls are counted.", "The line exposes crooked rolling and sideways hand pushes instantly.", "Hands must land back on the line, palms down, beside the ears.", "Ten counted rolls per session; crooked rolls are repeated, not counted.", "The line rule holds from crouch starts through standing starts."], "requirements": ["A mat with a straight tape line.", "The basic backward roll already learned."], "standards": ["Ten rolls are counted with the line kept on every one.", "The hands land on the line beside the ears on the checked reps.", "Standing-start rolls keep the line."], "citations": ["Reid H and Croucher M. The Way of the Warrior: The Paradox of the Martial Arts. Overlook Press; 1983.", "Smith R W. Chinese Boxing: Masters and Methods. North Atlantic Books; 1974."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 10, "ladder_tier": "compounded", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"The Signature Back Roll Line - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000010 [Falling and Rolling]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # back roll line: line rule, counted rolls, hand audit\n    line_length_ft = 8.0\n    counted_rolls = 10\n    hand_land = \"on the line, palms down, beside the ears\"\n    starts = [\"crouch\", \"standing\"]\n    print(\"line: %.0f ft straight tape\" % line_length_ft)\n    print(\"counted rolls: %d\" % counted_rolls)\n    print(\"hand audit: %s\" % hand_land)\n    print(\"starts: %s\" % \", \".join(starts))\n    assert counted_rolls == 10\n    assert len(starts) == 2\n    print(\"rule: only on-line rolls are counted\")\n    print(\"OK: Signature back roll holds the line\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000011", "t": "Signature Side-Fall Plane", "g": "Falling and Rolling", "name": "Signature Side-Fall Plane", "title": "Signature Side-Fall Plane", "description": "The Signature Side-Fall Plane rebuilds the side breakfall around a single image: the body is a plane landing all at once. The student practices against a wall, tipping sideways from kneeling with the slapping arm pre-positioned, and a partner claps the instant of landing - the slap, thigh, hip, and side must all arrive within the same clap. This Signature-generated original instruction adds the exhale count: the student shouts ha on impact, and a silent landing is a failed rep, because the shout proves the breath braced the ribs. Ten planes per side per session, all shouted, all simultaneous.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: kneel beside a wall with the slapping arm pre-positioned, slightly bent. Step 2: tip sideways like a falling plane, keeping the body straight. Step 3: slap the mat with the forearm a fraction before the body arrives. Step 4: land thigh, hip, side, and arm together while shouting ha. Step 5: the partner claps the landing instant - all contacts must fall inside the one clap. Step 6: drill ten per side, every rep shouted. Step 7: progress to crouch and standing starts with a partner push, keeping the shout and the simultaneity. The common errors are landing in sequence instead of at once, which the clap exposes, shouting after the landing instead of on it, and pre-positioning the arm too far out, which strains the shoulder.", "key_points": ["The body lands as a plane: slap, thigh, hip, side all inside one clap.", "The shout ha on impact proves the breath braced the ribs; silent reps fail.", "The slapping arm pre-positions slightly bent, never straight.", "Ten shouted planes per side per session.", "The clap test holds from kneeling through standing starts."], "requirements": ["A mat beside a wall and a partner with the clap test.", "The basic side fall already learned."], "standards": ["Ten planes per side land inside the single clap.", "Every rep is shouted on impact, none silent.", "Standing-start planes pass the clap test."], "citations": ["Smith R W. Chinese Boxing: Masters and Methods. North Atlantic Books; 1974.", "Reid H and Croucher M. The Way of the Warrior: The Paradox of the Martial Arts. Overlook Press; 1983."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 11, "ladder_tier": "compounded", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Signature Side-Fall Plane - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000011 [Falling and Rolling]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # side-fall plane: contacts, clap window, shout rule, reps\n    contacts = [\"forearm slap\", \"thigh\", \"hip\", \"side\"]\n    clap_window_ms = 120\n    shout = \"ha\"\n    planes_per_side = 10\n    print(\"contacts: %s\" % \" + \".join(contacts))\n    print(\"landing window: %d ms (one clap)\" % clap_window_ms)\n    print(\"shout: %s (silent rep fails)\" % shout)\n    print(\"planes per side: %d\" % planes_per_side)\n    total = planes_per_side * 2\n    print(\"total planes: %d\" % total)\n    assert total == 20\n    assert len(contacts) == 4\n    print(\"rule: the body is a plane landing all at once\")\n    print(\"OK: Signature side-fall lands as one plane\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000012", "t": "Kneel-Drop Safety Ladder", "g": "Falling and Rolling", "name": "Kneel-Drop Safety Ladder", "title": "Kneel-Drop Safety Ladder", "description": "The Kneel-Drop Safety Ladder is the JAH progression for the standing-to-kneeling drop: four rungs, each with a pass standard, climbed in order. Rung one: the drop onto a thick mat with hands lightly touching for balance - ten clean drops. Rung two: the same drop with the paws in guard, no hand touch - ten clean drops. Rung three: the drop on a thin mat with the acting - shoulders slump, head lolls - while the guard holds. Rung four: the drop with the instant rise, under two seconds down-and-up. This Signature-generated original instruction makes the drop a ladder: no rung is skipped, and each rung ten-for-ten before the next.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: climb rung one: ten drops on the thick mat with light hand touches allowed. Step 2: climb rung two: ten drops with paws in guard and zero hand touches. Step 3: climb rung three: ten drops on the thin mat with full acting and the guard holding. Step 4: climb rung four: ten drops with the rise, each down-and-up under two seconds. Step 5: a failed rep - hand touch on rung two, lost guard on rung three, over time on rung four - restarts the count for that rung. Step 6: log the rung reached and the date each rung is passed. Step 7: re-climb the ladder monthly to verify the skill has not decayed. The common errors are skipping rungs, counting failed reps, and never re-climbing, which lets the skill rot.", "key_points": ["Four rungs: hand-touch drops, guard drops, acting drops on thin mat, drops with instant rise.", "Each rung requires ten clean reps before the next rung is attempted.", "Failed reps restart the rung count; standards are not negotiated.", "Rung dates are logged; the ladder is re-climbed monthly.", "No rung is ever skipped."], "requirements": ["A thick mat and a thin mat, and a timer for rung four.", "The basic kneeling drop already learned.", "A training log for rung dates."], "standards": ["All four rungs are passed ten-for-ten in order.", "Rung four down-and-up runs under two seconds on every rep.", "The monthly re-climb is logged."], "citations": ["Chow D and Spangler R. Kung Fu: History, Philosophy, and Technique. Unique Publications; 1982.", "Wong Kiew Kit. The Art of Shaolin Kung Fu. Tuttle Publishing; 2002."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 12, "ladder_tier": "compounded", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Kneel-Drop Safety Ladder - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000012 [Falling and Rolling]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # safety ladder: rungs, reps, standards\n    rungs = [(\"hand-touch drops\", \"thick mat\", 10),\n             (\"guard drops\", \"thick mat\", 10),\n             (\"acting drops\", \"thin mat\", 10),\n             (\"drop with rise\", \"thin mat\", 10)]\n    rise_target_s = 2.0\n    print(\"rungs:\")\n    for i, (name, mat, reps) in enumerate(rungs, 1):\n        print(\"  rung %d: %-16s %s x%d\" % (i, name, mat, reps))\n    print(\"rung 4 rise target: under %.1f s\" % rise_target_s)\n    total = sum(r for _, _, r in rungs)\n    print(\"total clean reps: %d\" % total)\n    assert total == 40\n    assert len(rungs) == 4\n    print(\"rule: no rung skipped, no failed rep counted\")\n    print(\"OK: kneel-drop ladder climbed clean\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000013", "t": "The Signature Under-Roll", "g": "Falling and Rolling", "name": "The Signature Under-Roll", "title": "The Signature Under-Roll", "description": "The Signature Under-Roll is the JAH escape roll: a forward roll with a measured exit. The student rolls under a rope held at head height - the rope is the strike line - and must exit the roll past a floor mark placed at forty-five degrees to the attack line. This Signature-generated original instruction makes the escape verifiable: the rope must not be touched, and the exit mark must be reached in stance. Partners raise the rope from waist height to head height over the weeks, and the exit mark moves from thirty to forty-five degrees, building the real escape geometry step by step.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: set the rope at waist height across the mat and mark the exit line at thirty degrees. Step 2: approach in the stance; as the partner calls now, tuck and roll under the rope. Step 3: exit the roll past the floor mark and rise into the stance facing the flank. Step 4: the rep counts only if the rope is untouched and the mark is reached. Step 5: drill ten counted reps, then raise the rope to chest height. Step 6: over the weeks raise the rope to head height and move the mark to forty-five degrees. Step 7: add the counter strike from the rise on the final reps. The common errors are ducking under the rope instead of rolling, which teaches the wrong escape, and counting reps that missed the mark.", "key_points": ["The roll passes under a rope - the strike line - without touching it.", "The exit mark sits at forty-five degrees to the attack line; the rise faces the flank.", "Only untouched-rope, on-mark reps are counted.", "The rope rises waist to head height and the mark moves thirty to forty-five degrees over weeks.", "The counter strike launches from the rise with no pause."], "requirements": ["A mat, a rope or belt held at adjustable height, and floor marks.", "A partner to call the roll and hold the rope.", "The forward roll already silent."], "standards": ["Ten counted reps clear the waist-height rope to the thirty-degree mark.", "The head-height rope is cleared to the forty-five-degree mark.", "Three reps finish with the counter strike from the rise."], "citations": ["Smith R W. Chinese Boxing: Masters and Methods. North Atlantic Books; 1974.", "Chow D and Spangler R. Kung Fu: History, Philosophy, and Technique. Unique Publications; 1982."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 13, "ladder_tier": "compounded", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"The Signature Under-Roll - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000013 [Falling and Rolling]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # under-roll: rope ladder, exit marks, counted reps\n    rope_ladder = [\"waist\", \"chest\", \"head\"]\n    mark_ladder = [30.0, 37.5, 45.0]\n    counted_reps = 10\n    print(\"rope ladder: %s\" % \" -> \".join(rope_ladder))\n    print(\"exit marks: %s deg\" % \", \".join(\"%.1f\" % m for m in mark_ladder))\n    print(\"counted reps per stage: %d\" % counted_reps)\n    total = counted_reps * len(rope_ladder)\n    print(\"total counted reps: %d\" % total)\n    assert total == 30\n    assert mark_ladder == sorted(mark_ladder)\n    print(\"rule: untouched rope, on-mark exit, or it does not count\")\n    print(\"OK: Signature under-roll escapes clean\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000014", "t": "Guard from the Floor, Signature Order", "g": "Falling and Rolling", "name": "Guard from the Floor, Signature Order", "title": "Guard from the Floor, Signature Order", "description": "Guard from the Floor, Signature Order, is the JAH chaining protocol: every breakfall ends in guard by a fixed order of operations. The order is: land, paws up, stance set, eyes up - four beats, always in that sequence, never rearranged. The student drills each of the three breakfalls to the four-beat order while a partner calls random beats out of order - the student must still execute land, paws, stance, eyes. This Signature-generated original instruction makes the chain disruption-proof: the order is the order regardless of what the fall or the opponent does. Thirty chains per session, ten per fall, all four beats verified.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: memorize the order: land, paws up, stance set, eyes up. Step 2: perform the forward roll and execute the four beats in order. Step 3: perform the backward roll and the side fall the same way. Step 4: the partner calls beats out of order - eyes, land, stance - and the student still executes the fixed order. Step 5: drill ten chains per fall, thirty total per session. Step 6: the partner verifies each beat: paws visibly up before the stance sets, eyes up last. Step 7: log the chains and any beat skipped. The common errors are setting the stance before the paws are up, dropping the eyes to the floor, and letting the partner calls rearrange the order.", "key_points": ["The fixed order: land, paws up, stance set, eyes up - never rearranged.", "The order holds even when the partner calls beats out of sequence.", "All three breakfalls chain through the same four beats.", "Thirty chains per session, ten per fall, every beat verified.", "Skipped beats are logged and the chain is repeated."], "requirements": ["A mat and a partner for the disruption calls.", "All three breakfalls already learned."], "standards": ["Thirty chains are completed with all four beats in order.", "The disruption test is passed: the order never rearranges.", "The log shows zero skipped beats on the final session."], "citations": ["Reid H and Croucher M. The Way of the Warrior: The Paradox of the Martial Arts. Overlook Press; 1983.", "Smith R W. Chinese Boxing: Masters and Methods. North Atlantic Books; 1974."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 14, "ladder_tier": "compounded", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Guard from the Floor, Signature Order - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000014 [Falling and Rolling]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # chaining protocol: fixed order, falls, disruption test\n    order = [\"land\", \"paws up\", \"stance set\", \"eyes up\"]\n    falls = [\"forward roll\", \"backward roll\", \"side fall\"]\n    chains_per_fall = 10\n    print(\"order: %s\" % \" -> \".join(order))\n    print(\"falls: %s\" % \", \".join(falls))\n    print(\"chains per fall: %d\" % chains_per_fall)\n    total = chains_per_fall * len(falls)\n    print(\"total chains: %d\" % total)\n    assert total == 30\n    assert len(order) == 4\n    print(\"rule: the order is the order, regardless of disruption\")\n    print(\"OK: guard from the floor locked in Signature order\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000015", "t": "The Signature Loose Whip", "g": "Deceptive Hand Strikes", "name": "The Signature Loose Whip", "title": "The Signature Loose Whip", "description": "The Signature Loose Whip is the JAH swinging-strike method: the strike is built as a three-link whip - shoulder, elbow, wrist - with each link timed to fire in sequence. The student practices the wave on a rope first: holding a short rope and snapping it so the wave travels to the tip teaches the sequential timing better than any explanation. Then the arm repeats the wave on the heavy bag, shoulder rolling, elbow unfolding, wrist snapping, each link adding speed to the last. This Signature-generated original instruction makes the loose strike a wave: fifty waves per arm per session, timed link by link.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: hold a short rope and practice snapping the wave to the tip, ten snaps. Step 2: stand in the stance and repeat the wave with the empty arm: shoulder rolls, elbow unfolds, wrist snaps. Step 3: practice the wave slowly, feeling each link fire in sequence. Step 4: take the wave to the heavy bag, letting the wrist snap at impact. Step 5: drill fifty waves per arm, alternating arms. Step 6: film or have the partner watch for the sequence: a link firing early is visible as a kink. Step 7: log the sessions and any kink noticed. The common errors are firing all links at once, which is just a stiff swing, snapping the wrist before the elbow unfolds, and muscling the shoulder, which kills the wave.", "key_points": ["The strike is a three-link wave: shoulder rolls, elbow unfolds, wrist snaps, in sequence.", "Rope-snapping practice teaches the sequential timing before the arm attempts it.", "Each link adds speed to the last; a kink means a link fired early.", "Fifty waves per arm per session on the heavy bag.", "A partner or camera checks the link sequence honestly."], "requirements": ["A heavy bag and a short rope for the wave practice.", "The basic loose-arm strike already learned."], "standards": ["Fifty waves per arm are completed with the visible link sequence.", "The partner confirms no kink - no early link - on the checked reps.", "Bag impact matches the student best stiff punch."], "citations": ["Smith R W. Chinese Boxing: Masters and Methods. North Atlantic Books; 1974.", "Chow D and Spangler R. Kung Fu: History, Philosophy, and Technique. Unique Publications; 1982."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 15, "ladder_tier": "compounded", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"The Signature Loose Whip - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000015 [Deceptive Hand Strikes]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # loose whip: three links, sequence, waves\n    links = [\"shoulder roll\", \"elbow unfold\", \"wrist snap\"]\n    rope_snaps = 10\n    waves_per_arm = 50\n    print(\"links in order: %s\" % \" -> \".join(links))\n    print(\"rope snaps: %d\" % rope_snaps)\n    print(\"waves per arm: %d\" % waves_per_arm)\n    total = waves_per_arm * 2\n    print(\"total waves: %d\" % total)\n    assert total == 100\n    assert len(links) == 3\n    print(\"rule: the strike is a wave, not a swing\")\n    print(\"OK: Signature loose whip cracks link by link\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000016", "t": "The Signature Low Pluck", "g": "Deceptive Hand Strikes", "name": "The Signature Low Pluck", "title": "The Signature Low Pluck", "description": "The Signature Low Pluck is the JAH rebuild of the famous low-grab technique: the misdirection is engineered as a two-channel signal. Channel one, the high channel: both paws rise wide with a loud exhale, pulling eyes and guard up. Channel two, the low channel: the plucking paw moves in the shadow of the lurch, timed to the exact beat the high paws reach their peak. Students drill the channels separately - ten high-channel sells, ten low-channel plucks - then combine them, with a partner scoring each rep: did the eyes stay up. This Signature-generated original instruction makes misdirection a scored skill: the partner scorecard, not the student feeling, decides when the technique is ready.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: drill the high channel alone: paws rise wide with the loud exhale, ten reps, selling upward. Step 2: drill the low channel alone: from the lurch, the paw darts low to the pad and snaps back, ten reps. Step 3: combine the channels: high paws peak as the low paw darts, ten reps slow. Step 4: the partner scores each rep: eyes stayed up, or eyes dropped. Step 5: drill until eight of ten reps score eyes-up. Step 6: increase speed, keeping the score standard. Step 7: log the scores each session. The common errors are combining the channels before each is clean, accepting unscored practice, and looking down, which fails the scorecard instantly.", "key_points": ["Two channels: the loud high sell and the quiet low pluck in its shadow.", "The low paw moves on the exact beat the high paws peak.", "Channels are drilled separately before they are combined.", "The partner scorecard - eyes stayed up - decides readiness, not feeling.", "Eight of ten eyes-up reps is the passing standard."], "requirements": ["A partner with a low pad and a scorecard.", "A mat for the low work."], "standards": ["Ten clean high-channel and ten clean low-channel reps are drilled separately.", "Eight of ten combined reps score eyes-up.", "The standard holds at increased speed."], "citations": ["Chow D and Spangler R. Kung Fu: History, Philosophy, and Technique. Unique Publications; 1982.", "Smith R W. Chinese Boxing: Masters and Methods. North Atlantic Books; 1974."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 16, "ladder_tier": "compounded", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"The Signature Low Pluck - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000016 [Deceptive Hand Strikes]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # low pluck: channels, scoring, standard\n    channels = [\"high sell\", \"low pluck\"]\n    separate_reps = 10\n    combined_reps = 10\n    passes_needed = 8\n    print(\"channels: %s\" % \" + \".join(channels))\n    print(\"separate reps per channel: %d\" % separate_reps)\n    print(\"combined reps: %d\" % combined_reps)\n    print(\"eyes-up passes needed: %d/%d\" % (passes_needed, combined_reps))\n    total = separate_reps * 2 + combined_reps\n    print(\"total reps: %d\" % total)\n    assert total == 30\n    assert passes_needed / combined_reps == 0.8\n    print(\"rule: the scorecard decides, not the feeling\")\n    print(\"OK: Signature low pluck scored and passed\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000017", "t": "Signature Misdirection Pair", "g": "Deceptive Hand Strikes", "name": "Signature Misdirection Pair", "title": "Signature Misdirection Pair", "description": "The Signature Misdirection Pair engineers the double-hand technique as a timing instrument: the loud hand and the quiet hand are separated by a counted beat. The student practices with a metronome at sixty beats per minute: the loud slap lands on the beat, the quiet hand lands exactly one beat later - no earlier, no later. This Signature-generated original instruction removes the guesswork from the split: the beat is the split. Partners hold the two pads and call early or late on the quiet hand until ten consecutive pairs land on the counted beat.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: set the metronome to sixty beats per minute before the two pads. Step 2: throw the wide looping slap to land on the beat. Step 3: throw the quiet straight hand to land exactly one beat later. Step 4: the partner calls early or late on every quiet hand. Step 5: drill until ten consecutive pairs land on the counted beat. Step 6: switch the hands and repeat the ten. Step 7: remove the metronome and keep the beat internally for ten more pairs, the partner still calling. The common errors are letting the quiet hand drift early, which the partner must catch, rushing the loud hand off the beat, and counting ten non-consecutive good pairs instead of ten in a row.", "key_points": ["The loud hand lands on the beat; the quiet hand lands exactly one beat later.", "The metronome at sixty beats per minute makes the split countable.", "The partner calls early or late on every quiet hand.", "Ten consecutive on-beat pairs per hand arrangement is the standard.", "The beat is kept internally after the metronome is removed."], "requirements": ["Two pads, a partner, and a metronome.", "The basic misdirection pair already learned."], "standards": ["Ten consecutive pairs land on the counted beat with each hand arrangement.", "Ten more pairs hold the beat with the metronome removed.", "The partner reports no early or late quiet hands in the final set."], "citations": ["Smith R W. Chinese Boxing: Masters and Methods. North Atlantic Books; 1974.", "Reid H and Croucher M. The Way of the Warrior: The Paradox of the Martial Arts. Overlook Press; 1983."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 17, "ladder_tier": "compounded", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Signature Misdirection Pair - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000017 [Deceptive Hand Strikes]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # misdirection pair: metronome, beat split, consecutive standard\n    bpm = 60\n    beat_gap = 1              # beats between loud and quiet hand\n    consecutive = 10\n    arrangements = 2\n    internal = 10\n    print(\"metronome: %d bpm\" % bpm)\n    print(\"beat gap: %d beat\" % beat_gap)\n    print(\"consecutive on-beat pairs: %d x %d arrangements\" % (consecutive, arrangements))\n    print(\"internal-beat pairs: %d\" % internal)\n    total = consecutive * arrangements + internal\n    print(\"total pairs: %d\" % total)\n    assert total == 30\n    assert beat_gap == 1\n    print(\"rule: the beat is the split\")\n    print(\"OK: Signature misdirection pair timed to the beat\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000018", "t": "The Signature Drop Punch", "g": "Deceptive Hand Strikes", "name": "The Signature Drop Punch", "title": "The Signature Drop Punch", "description": "The Signature Drop Punch rebuilds the falling punch around impact timing: the punch must land within a fifty-millisecond window centered on the knee-touch instant. The student practices the drop beside a partner who claps at the knee touch; the punch is correct only if its impact sound falls inside the clap. This Signature-generated original instruction makes the falling punch a timing skill verified by ear: ten drops per side, and the rep counts only when the punch lands in the window. The acting - the genuine-looking collapse - is scored separately by the partner: collapse believable, or not.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: stand before the heavy bag and begin the kneeling drop. Step 2: drive the rear paw straight as the knee nears the floor. Step 3: the partner claps at the knee-touch instant; the punch impact must fall inside the clap. Step 4: count only the reps where punch and clap coincide. Step 5: drill ten counted reps per side. Step 6: the partner separately scores the acting: believable collapse or not, ten scores. Step 7: log the timing passes and the acting scores. The common errors are punching early and calling it close enough, dropping forward instead of straight down, and neglecting the acting score, which is half the technique.", "key_points": ["The punch must land inside a fifty-millisecond window at the knee-touch instant.", "The partner clap marks the window; only coincident reps are counted.", "Ten counted reps per side make the timing reliable.", "The acting is scored separately: believable collapse or not.", "Timing passes and acting scores are both logged."], "requirements": ["A heavy bag, a mat, and a partner with the clap timing.", "The kneeling drop already learned."], "standards": ["Ten coincident reps per side are counted.", "Eight of ten acting scores read as believable collapse.", "The log shows both standards met."], "citations": ["Chow D and Spangler R. Kung Fu: History, Philosophy, and Technique. Unique Publications; 1982.", "Smith R W. Chinese Boxing: Masters and Methods. North Atlantic Books; 1974."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 18, "ladder_tier": "compounded", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"The Signature Drop Punch - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000018 [Deceptive Hand Strikes]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # drop punch: timing window, counted reps, acting score\n    window_ms = 50\n    counted_per_side = 10\n    acting_scores = 10\n    acting_passes = 8\n    print(\"timing window: %d ms at knee touch\" % window_ms)\n    print(\"counted reps per side: %d\" % counted_per_side)\n    print(\"acting scores: %d, passes needed: %d\" % (acting_scores, acting_passes))\n    total = counted_per_side * 2\n    print(\"total counted reps: %d\" % total)\n    assert total == 20\n    assert acting_passes / acting_scores == 0.8\n    print(\"rule: the punch lands in the window or it does not count\")\n    print(\"OK: Signature drop punch timed to the fall\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000019", "t": "The Signature False Stumble", "g": "Deceptive Hand Strikes", "name": "The Signature False Stumble", "title": "The Signature False Stumble", "description": "The Signature False Stumble engineers the stumble feint as a three-part trigger: the lurch, the load, and the launch. The student practices with a pressure pad under the far foot - a folded towel that compresses: the lurch is correct only if the towel compresses, proving the weight truly loaded the far leg. This Signature-generated original instruction makes the feint honest: the stumble must be a real weight shift, because only a real shift can launch a real counter. Ten triggered feints per side, the towel compressing every time, then the partner punch is added.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: place the folded towel under the far foot and take the stance. Step 2: lurch to the side, loading the far leg until the towel compresses. Step 3: check the compression - no compression, no rep. Step 4: plant and fire the counter from the loaded leg. Step 5: drill ten towel-verified feints per side. Step 6: add the partner straight punch; slip it inside the lurch and counter. Step 7: remove the towel after two weeks and keep the loading honest by feel, spot-checked with the towel. The common errors are faking the lurch without loading, which the towel exposes, firing before the load completes, and counting uncompressed reps.", "key_points": ["The lurch must compress the towel under the far foot - proof of the real weight shift.", "No compression means no rep; the feint must be honest to work.", "The counter launches only from the loaded leg, after the load completes.", "Ten verified feints per side before the partner punch is added.", "Towel spot-checks keep the loading honest after the towel is removed."], "requirements": ["A folded towel as the pressure pad and a mat.", "A partner for the punch stage.", "The basic stumble feint already learned."], "standards": ["Ten towel-verified feints per side compress the towel every time.", "Five partner-punch reps slip and counter from the loaded leg.", "Spot-checks after towel removal still show compression."], "citations": ["Reid H and Croucher M. The Way of the Warrior: The Paradox of the Martial Arts. Overlook Press; 1983.", "Chow D and Spangler R. Kung Fu: History, Philosophy, and Technique. Unique Publications; 1982."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 19, "ladder_tier": "compounded", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"The Signature False Stumble - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000019 [Deceptive Hand Strikes]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # false stumble: load verification, reps, partner stage\n    verified_per_side = 10\n    compression_required = True\n    partner_reps = 5\n    print(\"towel-verified feints per side: %d\" % verified_per_side)\n    print(\"compression required: %s\" % compression_required)\n    print(\"partner punch reps: %d\" % partner_reps)\n    total = verified_per_side * 2 + partner_reps\n    print(\"total reps: %d\" % total)\n    assert total == 25\n    assert compression_required is True\n    print(\"rule: the stumble must be honest to launch the counter\")\n    print(\"OK: Signature false stumble loads and fires\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000020", "t": "Signature Crouch Elbow", "g": "Deceptive Hand Strikes", "name": "Signature Crouch Elbow", "title": "Signature Crouch Elbow", "description": "Signature Crouch Elbow is the JAH rising-elbow method: the elbow is driven by a counted leg explosion. The student crouches on a scale - a bathroom scale under the front foot - and practices the rise: the scale reading must spike as the legs drive, proving the power came from the legs and not the arm. This Signature-generated original instruction makes leg drive measurable: the scale spike is the technique. Ten spiked rises per side per session, each finishing with the elbow through the pad, build the honest rising elbow.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: place the scale under the front foot and settle into the crouch before the pad. Step 2: chamber the elbow low by the ribs. Step 3: explode upward through the legs, watching the scale spike. Step 4: let the rising body carry the elbow through the pad in the tight arc. Step 5: count only the reps with a visible scale spike - no spike, no rep. Step 6: drill ten counted reps per side. Step 7: remove the scale after two weeks and keep the explosion honest by feel, spot-checked with the scale. The common errors are muscling the elbow, which shows no spike, rising slowly, and counting spikeless reps.", "key_points": ["A scale under the front foot measures the leg drive: the spike proves the legs fired.", "No spike means no rep; arm-muscled elbows are exposed instantly.", "Ten spiked rises per side per session.", "The elbow travels the tight upward arc through the pad on every counted rep.", "Scale spot-checks keep the explosion honest after the scale is removed."], "requirements": ["A bathroom scale, a pad with a holder, and a mat.", "The basic crouch elbow already learned."], "standards": ["Ten spiked reps per side are counted with visible scale spikes.", "The pad holder confirms the tight arc on every rep.", "Spot-checks after scale removal still show spikes."], "citations": ["Smith R W. Chinese Boxing: Masters and Methods. North Atlantic Books; 1974.", "Chow D and Spangler R. Kung Fu: History, Philosophy, and Technique. Unique Publications; 1982."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 20, "ladder_tier": "compounded", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Signature Crouch Elbow - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000020 [Deceptive Hand Strikes]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # crouch elbow: scale spike standard, reps\n    body_kg = 70.0\n    spike_min_kg = 1.5 * body_kg   # scale must read 1.5x body weight at explosion\n    counted_per_side = 10\n    print(\"body: %.0f kg\" % body_kg)\n    print(\"spike standard: %.0f kg on the scale\" % spike_min_kg)\n    print(\"counted reps per side: %d\" % counted_per_side)\n    total = counted_per_side * 2\n    print(\"total reps: %d\" % total)\n    assert spike_min_kg == 105.0\n    assert total == 20\n    print(\"rule: the scale spike is the technique\")\n    print(\"OK: Signature crouch elbow explodes from the legs\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000021", "t": "The Signature Floor Sweep", "g": "Ground Techniques", "name": "The Signature Floor Sweep", "title": "The Signature Floor Sweep", "description": "The Signature Floor Sweep rebuilds the crouch sweep as a measured arc: the sweeping foot must travel along a floor tape arc of ninety degrees, and the rep counts only if the foot stays on the tape. The student also learns the Signature low-line rule: the sweep is only thrown when the opponent weight is on the target leg, read from the partner ankle - the ankle bone visibly loads. This Signature-generated original instruction makes the sweep a two-verification skill: the tape verifies the arc, the ankle-read verifies the timing. Ten verified sweeps per leg per session.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: lay the ninety-degree tape arc on the mat and crouch at its center. Step 2: watch the partner ankle until the bone visibly loads - the weight is on the target leg. Step 3: sweep the foot along the tape arc across the ankle line. Step 4: count the rep only if the foot stayed on the tape and the ankle was loaded. Step 5: retract instantly to the crouch. Step 6: drill ten verified sweeps per leg. Step 7: log the verified count and any mistimed reads. The common errors are sweeping an unloaded leg, which the ankle-read prevents, leaving the tape arc, and leaving the leg extended after the sweep.", "key_points": ["The sweeping foot travels a ninety-degree floor tape arc; off-tape reps do not count.", "The sweep is thrown only on the loaded ankle, read from the visible ankle load.", "Two verifications per rep: the tape for the arc, the ankle-read for the timing.", "Ten verified sweeps per leg per session.", "Instant retraction to the crouch after every sweep."], "requirements": ["Floor tape for the arc, a mat, and a partner in shin pads.", "The basic crouch sweep already learned."], "standards": ["Ten verified sweeps per leg stay on the tape with the ankle loaded.", "The partner confirms the timing reads on most reps.", "Every sweep retracts with balance intact."], "citations": ["Smith R W. Chinese Boxing: Masters and Methods. North Atlantic Books; 1974.", "Chow D and Spangler R. Kung Fu: History, Philosophy, and Technique. Unique Publications; 1982."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 21, "ladder_tier": "compounded", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"The Signature Floor Sweep - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000021 [Ground Techniques]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # floor sweep: tape arc, ankle-read timing, verified reps\n    arc_deg = 90.0\n    verified_per_leg = 10\n    verifications = [\"tape arc\", \"ankle loaded\"]\n    print(\"tape arc: %.0f deg\" % arc_deg)\n    print(\"verifications: %s\" % \" + \".join(verifications))\n    print(\"verified sweeps per leg: %d\" % verified_per_leg)\n    total = verified_per_leg * 2\n    print(\"total verified sweeps: %d\" % total)\n    assert total == 20\n    assert len(verifications) == 2\n    print(\"rule: the tape verifies the arc, the ankle verifies the moment\")\n    print(\"OK: Signature floor sweep verified twice per rep\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000022", "t": "Signature Supine Rolling Kick", "g": "Ground Techniques", "name": "Signature Supine Rolling Kick", "title": "Signature Supine Rolling Kick", "description": "Signature Supine Rolling Kick is the JAH back-kick method: the kick is measured by a hanging target. A pad hangs from a rope at shin height over the student; the kick counts only if the pad swings - proof of solid contact - and the student calls the target before kicking: shin, knee, or thigh. This Signature-generated original instruction makes the supine kick a called shot: ten called kicks per leg, each one named before it is thrown, each one swinging the pad. The hip-drive rule holds: the pad must swing from hip power, and a partner watches that the leg alone never does the work.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: lie on the back under the hanging pad with the chin tucked and guard up. Step 2: call the target aloud: shin, knee, or thigh. Step 3: chamber and thrust the leg, driving from the hips. Step 4: count the rep only if the pad swings from the contact. Step 5: snap back to chamber and call the next target. Step 6: drill ten called kicks per leg. Step 7: chain the kick-to-rise: called kick, roll, rise to guard, five chains. The common errors are kicking without calling, which removes the aim training, accepting pad touches that do not swing it, and letting the leg do the work without the hips.", "key_points": ["The pad must swing: only swinging contact counts as a solid kick.", "Every kick is a called shot - the target named before the leg moves.", "Ten called kicks per leg per session.", "Hip drive is verified by a partner; leg-only kicks do not count.", "The kick chains to the rise: called kick, roll, rise to guard."], "requirements": ["A mat, a pad hung from a rope at shin height, and a partner.", "The basic supine kick already learned."], "standards": ["Ten called kicks per leg swing the pad.", "The partner confirms hip drive on the checked reps.", "Five kick-to-rise chains flow cleanly."], "citations": ["Reid H and Croucher M. The Way of the Warrior: The Paradox of the Martial Arts. Overlook Press; 1983.", "Smith R W. Chinese Boxing: Masters and Methods. North Atlantic Books; 1974."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 22, "ladder_tier": "compounded", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Signature Supine Rolling Kick - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000022 [Ground Techniques]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # supine kick: called shots, swing standard, chains\n    called_per_leg = 10\n    targets = [\"shin\", \"knee\", \"thigh\"]\n    chains = 5\n    swing_required = True\n    print(\"called kicks per leg: %d\" % called_per_leg)\n    print(\"targets: %s\" % \", \".join(targets))\n    print(\"swing required: %s\" % swing_required)\n    print(\"kick-to-rise chains: %d\" % chains)\n    total = called_per_leg * 2\n    print(\"total called kicks: %d\" % total)\n    assert total == 20\n    assert len(targets) == 3\n    print(\"rule: called, swung, and chained - or it does not count\")\n    print(\"OK: Signature supine kick lands called shots\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000023", "t": "The Signature Rising Kick Guard", "g": "Ground Techniques", "name": "The Signature Rising Kick Guard", "title": "The Signature Rising Kick Guard", "description": "The Signature Rising Kick Guard turns the supine defense into a counted rhythm drill: the student alternates kicks to a metronome at ninety beats per minute, one kick per beat, while a partner advances and retreats. The metronome exposes the two failures: rushing, which breaks the alternation, and stalling, which lets the partner close distance. This Signature-generated original instruction makes the defense a rhythm: two minutes on the metronome, kicks alternating every beat, distance held at two leg-lengths. The rise is practiced on the partner retreat: two kicks, then the roll and rise, five times.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: lie on the back with the metronome at ninety beats per minute. Step 2: alternate heel thrusts one per beat at the partner pads. Step 3: hold the distance at two leg-lengths; the partner calls close if it shrinks. Step 4: keep the rhythm for two minutes without rushing or stalling. Step 5: on the partner retreat, throw two kicks, roll, and rise to guard - five times. Step 6: rest one minute and repeat the two-minute round. Step 7: log the rounds and any distance breaks. The common errors are kicking off-beat, which the metronome exposes, letting both legs extend together, and missing the rise window on the retreat.", "key_points": ["Alternating kicks run one per beat at ninety beats per minute - the rhythm is the defense.", "Distance holds at two leg-lengths; the partner calls any close.", "Two-minute rounds with a one-minute rest, rhythm unbroken.", "The rise launches on the partner retreat: two kicks, roll, rise.", "Rounds and distance breaks are logged."], "requirements": ["A mat, a metronome, and a partner in shin pads.", "The basic supine defense already learned."], "standards": ["Two full two-minute rounds hold the beat and the distance.", "Five retreat-rises finish in guard cleanly.", "The log shows no distance breaks in the final round."], "citations": ["Smith R W. Chinese Boxing: Masters and Methods. North Atlantic Books; 1974.", "Reid H and Croucher M. The Way of the Warrior: The Paradox of the Martial Arts. Overlook Press; 1983."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 23, "ladder_tier": "compounded", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"The Signature Rising Kick Guard - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000023 [Ground Techniques]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # rising kick guard: metronome rhythm, rounds, distance, rises\n    bpm = 90\n    round_s = 120\n    rounds = 2\n    distance_leg_lengths = 2.0\n    retreat_rises = 5\n    print(\"metronome: %d bpm, one kick per beat\" % bpm)\n    print(\"rounds: %d x %d s\" % (rounds, round_s))\n    print(\"distance: %.0f leg-lengths\" % distance_leg_lengths)\n    print(\"retreat rises: %d\" % retreat_rises)\n    kicks = int(rounds * round_s / (60.0 / bpm))\n    print(\"total kicks: %d\" % kicks)\n    assert kicks == 360\n    assert retreat_rises == 5\n    print(\"rule: the rhythm holds the distance, the retreat opens the rise\")\n    print(\"OK: Signature rising kick guard keeps time and space\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000024", "t": "Sweep-to-Stand Signature Chain", "g": "Ground Techniques", "name": "Sweep-to-Stand Signature Chain", "title": "Sweep-to-Stand Signature Chain", "description": "The Sweep-to-Stand Signature Chain is the JAH combination protocol: sweep, plant, rise, strike - four beats on a two-second timer. The student performs the chain while a partner times it: the full four beats must complete inside two seconds, and the strike must land on the pad. This Signature-generated original instruction makes the combination a timed event: ten chains per side, each inside two seconds, each ending with the strike on the pad. A chain over time or with a missed pad is repeated. The chain is the style in miniature: low attack becomes upright guard becomes strike, with no beat given away.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: crouch at sweep range before the partner and the strike pad. Step 2: on the timer start, sweep the ankle line. Step 3: plant the sweeping foot and rise. Step 4: fire the loose-arm strike at the pad as the stance sets. Step 5: stop the timer; the four beats must fit inside two seconds. Step 6: count only the chains inside two seconds with the strike on the pad. Step 7: drill ten counted chains per side, logging times. The common errors are pausing after the sweep, which blows the timer, rising with the hands down, and accepting over-time chains.", "key_points": ["Four beats - sweep, plant, rise, strike - inside two seconds.", "The timer decides: over-time chains are repeated, not counted.", "The strike must land on the pad as the stance sets.", "Ten counted chains per side per session, all timed.", "Times are logged; the chain trends faster over the weeks."], "requirements": ["A mat, a partner, a strike pad, and a timer.", "The crouch sweep and the loose strike already learned."], "standards": ["Ten chains per side complete inside two seconds with the strike on the pad.", "No pause appears between any two beats.", "The log shows the average time dropping over four weeks."], "citations": ["Chow D and Spangler R. Kung Fu: History, Philosophy, and Technique. Unique Publications; 1982.", "Smith R W. Chinese Boxing: Masters and Methods. North Atlantic Books; 1974."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 24, "ladder_tier": "compounded", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Sweep-to-Stand Signature Chain - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000024 [Ground Techniques]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # sweep-to-stand chain: four beats, timer, counted chains\n    beats = [\"sweep\", \"plant\", \"rise\", \"strike\"]\n    time_limit_s = 2.0\n    counted_per_side = 10\n    print(\"beats: %s\" % \" -> \".join(beats))\n    print(\"time limit: %.1f s\" % time_limit_s)\n    print(\"counted chains per side: %d\" % counted_per_side)\n    total = counted_per_side * 2\n    print(\"total chains: %d\" % total)\n    assert total == 20\n    assert len(beats) == 4\n    print(\"rule: four beats, two seconds, no beat given away\")\n    print(\"OK: Signature chain runs inside the timer\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000025", "t": "The Signature Falling Elbow", "g": "Ground Techniques", "name": "The Signature Falling Elbow", "title": "The Signature Falling Elbow", "description": "The Signature Falling Elbow rebuilds the ground elbow drop as a control-first skill: the student practices stopping the drop. A pad sits on the floor; the student drops the elbow toward it but must stop one inch above the pad on the partner call stop - ten stops per side - before any full-contact drops are allowed. This Signature-generated original instruction makes control the prerequisite: only students who can stop the drop may deliver it. After the stop standard is passed, ten full drops per side are drilled on the pad, each one stoppable in principle, each one logged.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: kneel beside the floor pad with the elbow chambered. Step 2: on the partner call, drop the elbow but stop one inch above the pad - the partner checks the gap. Step 3: drill ten stops per side; the gap must hold every time. Step 4: only after ten-for-ten stops, drill ten full drops per side on the pad. Step 5: mix called stops into the full drops: the student never knows which is coming. Step 6: log the stops and the drops separately. Step 7: practice is on the pad only, never on a partner - the rule is absolute. The common errors are attempting full drops before passing the stop standard, stopping with a bent wrist instead of a vertical elbow, and practicing on anything but the pad.", "key_points": ["Control is the prerequisite: ten stoppable drops before any full-contact drop.", "The stop holds the elbow one inch above the pad, verified by the partner.", "Called stops are mixed into full drops; the student never knows which comes.", "Ten full drops per side follow the stop standard, all on the pad.", "Pad-only practice is absolute - never a partner."], "requirements": ["A floor pad, a mat, and a partner for the stop calls.", "The basic kneeling drop already learned."], "standards": ["Ten stops per side hold the one-inch gap every time.", "Ten full drops per side land with the elbow vertical.", "The mixed stop/drop drill is passed with no surprises."], "citations": ["Smith R W. Chinese Boxing: Masters and Methods. North Atlantic Books; 1974.", "Reid H and Croucher M. The Way of the Warrior: The Paradox of the Martial Arts. Overlook Press; 1983."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 25, "ladder_tier": "compounded", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"The Signature Falling Elbow - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000025 [Ground Techniques]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # falling elbow: stop standard, then full drops, mixed drill\n    stops_per_side = 10\n    stop_gap_in = 1.0\n    drops_per_side = 10\n    print(\"stops per side: %d at %.0f in gap\" % (stops_per_side, stop_gap_in))\n    print(\"full drops per side: %d (after stop standard)\" % drops_per_side)\n    total = (stops_per_side + drops_per_side) * 2\n    print(\"total reps: %d\" % total)\n    assert total == 40\n    assert stop_gap_in == 1.0\n    print(\"rule: control first, power second, pads always\")\n    print(\"OK: Signature falling elbow stops and delivers\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000026", "t": "Signature Ankle Hook", "g": "Ground Techniques", "name": "Signature Ankle Hook", "title": "Signature Ankle Hook", "description": "Signature Ankle Hook is the JAH trap method: the hook is timed to the partner step with a counted release. The student hooks the passing ankle and must release on the partner count of two - hook on one, release on two - because a held hook is a lost hook. A partner scores the timing: early release, late release, or on-count. This Signature-generated original instruction makes the trap a rhythm: ten hooks per side, each released on the two-count, each scored. The hook-to-rise chain follows: hook, release on two, roll, rise - five chains, all on the count.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: lie on the side as the partner steps slowly past. Step 2: on the partner one, hook the passing ankle with the instep. Step 3: on the partner two, release the hook - never hold past two. Step 4: the partner scores each rep: early, late, or on-count. Step 5: drill ten scored hooks per side; eight of ten must be on-count. Step 6: chain the hook-to-rise: hook, release on two, roll, rise - five chains. Step 7: log the scores and the chains. The common errors are holding past the two-count, hooking with the hands instead of the instep, and skipping the scored reps for unscored practice.", "key_points": ["Hook on one, release on two: the trap is a rhythm, never a hold.", "The partner scores every rep: early, late, or on-count.", "Eight of ten on-count reps per side is the passing standard.", "The instep hooks, never the hands.", "The hook-to-rise chain runs on the same count: five chains."], "requirements": ["A mat and a partner stepping slowly past in shin pads.", "The basic ankle hook already learned."], "standards": ["Ten scored hooks per side reach eight of ten on-count.", "Five hook-to-rise chains finish in guard on the count.", "The log shows the scores improving over two weeks."], "citations": ["Chow D and Spangler R. Kung Fu: History, Philosophy, and Technique. Unique Publications; 1982.", "Reid H and Croucher M. The Way of the Warrior: The Paradox of the Martial Arts. Overlook Press; 1983."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 26, "ladder_tier": "compounded", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Signature Ankle Hook - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000026 [Ground Techniques]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # ankle hook: count rhythm, scoring, chains\n    counts = [\"one: hook\", \"two: release\"]\n    scored_per_side = 10\n    passes_needed = 8\n    chains = 5\n    print(\"rhythm: %s\" % \", \".join(counts))\n    print(\"scored hooks per side: %d\" % scored_per_side)\n    print(\"on-count passes needed: %d/%d\" % (passes_needed, scored_per_side))\n    print(\"hook-to-rise chains: %d\" % chains)\n    total = scored_per_side * 2\n    print(\"total scored hooks: %d\" % total)\n    assert total == 20\n    assert passes_needed / scored_per_side == 0.8\n    print(\"rule: hook on one, release on two - a held hook is a lost hook\")\n    print(\"OK: Signature ankle hook timed and released\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000027", "t": "Signature Form: Road One", "g": "Classic Forms", "name": "Signature Form: Road One", "title": "Signature Form: Road One", "description": "Signature Form Road One is the JAH opening sequence: eight moves, each with a checkpoint, performed as a scored routine. The moves are the salute, stance and sway, crouch, drunken walk, paw strikes, kneeling drop and rise, closing sway, and salute - and a partner scores each move pass or fail against the checkpoint list from the earlier records. This Signature-generated original instruction makes the form a testable routine: the road is performed three times, and the score must reach twenty-two of twenty-four checkpoints - eight moves times three runs - before Road Two is taught. The form is the archive of the art, and the archive must be accurate.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: learn the eight moves in order from the earlier records. Step 2: link the moves into the road, slowly. Step 3: the partner holds the checkpoint list: one checkpoint per move. Step 4: perform the road three times while the partner scores each move. Step 5: total the score: twenty-two of twenty-four checkpoints is the pass. Step 6: failed checkpoints are drilled individually, then the road is re-scored. Step 7: log the scores; only a passed Road One unlocks Road Two. The common errors are rushing to Road Two on a failed score, scoring without the checkpoint list, and treating the form as performance before it is accurate.", "key_points": ["Eight moves, one checkpoint each, performed as a scored routine.", "Three runs, twenty-four checkpoints, twenty-two to pass.", "Failed checkpoints are drilled individually before re-scoring.", "A passed Road One unlocks Road Two - the sequence is gated.", "Scores are logged; the form is an accurate archive."], "requirements": ["Clear floor and a partner with the checkpoint list.", "All foundation techniques already learned."], "standards": ["Three scored runs reach twenty-two of twenty-four checkpoints.", "Every failed checkpoint is re-drilled and re-scored.", "The log shows the passing score before Road Two begins."], "citations": ["Wong Kiew Kit. The Art of Shaolin Kung Fu. Tuttle Publishing; 2002.", "Chow D and Spangler R. Kung Fu: History, Philosophy, and Technique. Unique Publications; 1982."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 27, "ladder_tier": "advanced", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Signature Form: Road One - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000027 [Classic Forms]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # road one: moves, checkpoints, runs, pass standard\n    moves = [\"salute\", \"stance and sway\", \"crouch\", \"drunken walk\", \"paw strikes\",\n             \"kneeling drop and rise\", \"closing sway\", \"salute\"]\n    runs = 3\n    checkpoints = len(moves) * runs\n    pass_mark = 22\n    print(\"moves: %d\" % len(moves))\n    print(\"runs: %d\" % runs)\n    print(\"checkpoints: %d\" % checkpoints)\n    print(\"pass mark: %d/%d\" % (pass_mark, checkpoints))\n    assert checkpoints == 24\n    assert pass_mark / checkpoints > 0.9\n    print(\"rule: the form is an archive, and the archive must be accurate\")\n    print(\"OK: Signature Road One scored and passed\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000028", "t": "Signature Form: Road Two", "g": "Classic Forms", "name": "Signature Form: Road Two", "title": "Signature Form: Road Two", "description": "Signature Form Road Two is the JAH middle sequence: the falling curriculum performed as a scored routine with a silence rule. The moves chain the forward roll, backward roll, side fall, monkey roll, and falling punch - and every roll must be silent, judged by the partner ear. This Signature-generated original instruction adds the noise audit: a roll that slaps or thumps fails its checkpoint regardless of shape, because in the Signature method a noisy fall is an unsafe fall. Three runs, twenty-four checkpoints, twenty-two to pass, same as Road One - and Road One must still be passed on demand.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: learn the Road Two moves in order from the falling records. Step 2: link the moves slowly, keeping every roll silent. Step 3: the partner scores each move with the noise audit: silent pass, noisy fail. Step 4: perform three scored runs. Step 5: total the score: twenty-two of twenty-four to pass. Step 6: re-perform Road One on demand to prove it is retained. Step 7: log both roads scores. The common errors are accepting noisy rolls, forgetting Road One while learning Road Two, and rushing the links before the falls are silent.", "key_points": ["The middle sequence chains the falling curriculum as a scored routine.", "The noise audit: a roll that slaps or thumps fails its checkpoint.", "Three runs, twenty-four checkpoints, twenty-two to pass.", "Road One must still be passed on demand - retention is tested.", "Both roads scores are logged."], "requirements": ["A long mat and a partner with the checkpoint list and the noise audit.", "Road One already passed."], "standards": ["Three Road Two runs reach twenty-two of twenty-four with silent rolls.", "Road One is re-passed on demand in the same session.", "The log shows both roads passing."], "citations": ["Chow D and Spangler R. Kung Fu: History, Philosophy, and Technique. Unique Publications; 1982.", "Smith R W. Chinese Boxing: Masters and Methods. North Atlantic Books; 1974."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 28, "ladder_tier": "advanced", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Signature Form: Road Two - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000028 [Classic Forms]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # road two: moves, noise audit, retention test\n    moves = [\"salute\", \"walk\", \"forward roll\", \"backward roll\", \"side fall\",\n             \"monkey roll\", \"falling punch\", \"salute\"]\n    runs = 3\n    checkpoints = len(moves) * runs\n    pass_mark = 22\n    retention = \"Road One re-passed on demand\"\n    print(\"moves: %d\" % len(moves))\n    print(\"checkpoints: %d, pass mark: %d\" % (checkpoints, pass_mark))\n    print(\"noise audit: silent pass, noisy fail\")\n    print(\"retention: %s\" % retention)\n    assert checkpoints == 24\n    assert pass_mark == 22\n    print(\"rule: a noisy fall is an unsafe fall\")\n    print(\"OK: Signature Road Two silent and retained\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000029", "t": "Signature Form: Road Three", "g": "Classic Forms", "name": "Signature Form: Road Three", "title": "Signature Form: Road Three", "description": "Signature Form Road Three is the JAH closing sequence: the deceptive and ground techniques performed as the finale, scored with an acting audit. The partner scores two columns: technique checkpoints and acting checkpoints - did the misdirection read as genuine, did the falls read as accidents. This Signature-generated original instruction makes the acting testable: the finale must deceive, not just execute. Three runs, twenty-four checkpoints per column, twenty-two to pass in each column. The closing salute is held two beats, timed by the partner, and the form ends only when all three roads have been performed back to back.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: learn the Road Three moves from the deceptive and ground records. Step 2: link the moves into the finale. Step 3: the partner scores two columns: technique and acting. Step 4: perform three scored runs. Step 5: pass twenty-two of twenty-four in each column. Step 6: hold the closing salute two full beats, timed. Step 7: perform all three roads back to back as the complete form, about five minutes. The common errors are scoring technique while ignoring acting, cutting the salute short, and never performing the three roads as one complete form.", "key_points": ["The finale sequences the deceptive and ground techniques as the closing road.", "Two scored columns: technique checkpoints and acting checkpoints.", "Twenty-two of twenty-four to pass in each column.", "The closing salute holds two full beats, timed by the partner.", "All three roads perform back to back as the complete five-minute form."], "requirements": ["Clear floor and a partner with the two-column scorecard.", "Roads One and Two already passed."], "standards": ["Three runs pass twenty-two of twenty-four in both columns.", "The closing salute holds two timed beats on every run.", "The complete three-road form is performed back to back."], "citations": ["Wong Kiew Kit. The Art of Shaolin Kung Fu. Tuttle Publishing; 2002.", "Reid H and Croucher M. The Way of the Warrior: The Paradox of the Martial Arts. Overlook Press; 1983."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 29, "ladder_tier": "advanced", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Signature Form: Road Three - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000029 [Classic Forms]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # road three: two columns, salute hold, complete form\n    columns = [\"technique\", \"acting\"]\n    checkpoints_each = 24\n    pass_each = 22\n    salute_beats = 2\n    complete_form_min = 5.0\n    print(\"columns: %s\" % \" + \".join(columns))\n    print(\"pass: %d/%d per column\" % (pass_each, checkpoints_each))\n    print(\"closing salute: %d beats\" % salute_beats)\n    print(\"complete form: ~%.0f min\" % complete_form_min)\n    total_checks = checkpoints_each * len(columns)\n    print(\"total checkpoints: %d\" % total_checks)\n    assert total_checks == 48\n    assert pass_each / checkpoints_each > 0.9\n    print(\"rule: the finale must deceive, not just execute\")\n    print(\"OK: Signature Road Three closes the form\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000030", "t": "The Signature Face Wash", "g": "Classic Forms", "name": "The Signature Face Wash", "title": "The Signature Face Wash", "description": "The Signature Face Wash is the JAH high defense: the brushing defense rebuilt with a coverage count. The student defends against a numbered volley - the partner calls three, then five, then seven high punches - and the wash must stay continuous through the whole called number with no reset and no punch getting through. This Signature-generated original instruction makes the defense a counted endurance skill: seven punches washed without a break is the standard. The acting - the lolling head inside the stumble - is scored separately: the partner reports whether the defense was visible or hidden.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: stand in the stance before the partner at punching range. Step 2: the partner calls three and throws three high straight punches. Step 3: wash all three continuously with the brushing motion, no reset. Step 4: progress to called volleys of five, then seven. Step 5: seven washed without a break is the standard. Step 6: the partner scores the acting: visible or hidden defense. Step 7: drill until seven are washed and the defense reads as hidden. The common errors are resetting between punches, which breaks the standard, swatting stiffly when tired, and dropping the acting under the volley pressure.", "key_points": ["The wash must stay continuous through a called volley: three, then five, then seven.", "Seven washed without a break or reset is the standard.", "No punch may get through during the volley.", "The acting is scored separately: the defense must read as hidden.", "The volley count is called before it is thrown - the defense knows what is coming."], "requirements": ["A partner throwing called high punch volleys.", "The basic face wash already learned."], "standards": ["Called volleys of three, five, and seven are all washed continuously.", "No punch gets through any volley.", "The partner scores the defense as hidden on the final volleys."], "citations": ["Chow D and Spangler R. Kung Fu: History, Philosophy, and Technique. Unique Publications; 1982.", "Smith R W. Chinese Boxing: Masters and Methods. North Atlantic Books; 1974."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 30, "ladder_tier": "advanced", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"The Signature Face Wash - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000030 [Classic Forms]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # face wash: called volleys, continuity standard, acting score\n    volleys = [3, 5, 7]\n    standard = 7\n    resets_allowed = 0\n    acting = \"hidden\"\n    print(\"called volleys: %s\" % volleys)\n    print(\"standard: %d washed, %d resets\" % (standard, resets_allowed))\n    print(\"acting score needed: %s\" % acting)\n    total = sum(volleys)\n    print(\"total punches washed: %d\" % total)\n    assert total == 15\n    assert standard == max(volleys)\n    print(\"rule: continuous through the called number, hidden inside the acting\")\n    print(\"OK: Signature face wash holds the high line\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000031", "t": "Signature Pole Climb", "g": "Classic Forms", "name": "Signature Pole Climb", "title": "Signature Pole Climb", "description": "Signature Pole Climb is the JAH rising-strike form piece: from the deep crouch the student rises as if climbing a pole hand over hand, and the climbing motion hides a rising elbow and a rising palm strike. The form piece is performed as a four-count: crouch on one, climb on two, elbow on three, palm on four - and the partner scores whether the strikes were visible before they launched. This Signature-generated original instruction makes the hidden strike testable: the climb must look like climbing until the strike lands. Ten climbs per session, strikes hidden on eight of ten.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: settle into the deep crouch. Step 2: count one: hold the crouch. Step 3: count two: rise hand over hand as if climbing a pole. Step 4: count three: the rising elbow fires from inside the climb. Step 5: count four: the rising palm follows. Step 6: the partner scores each climb: strikes hidden or seen early. Step 7: drill ten climbs; eight of ten must score hidden. The common errors are chambering the elbow before the climb, which the partner sees, climbing without rising power, and rushing the four-count.", "key_points": ["The four-count: crouch, climb, elbow, palm - the climb hides the strikes.", "The rising elbow and palm launch from inside the climbing motion.", "The partner scores hidden or seen-early on every climb.", "Eight of ten hidden is the passing standard.", "Ten climbs per session keep the piece sharp."], "requirements": ["A partner to score the hidden standard.", "The crouch elbow and basic palm strike already learned."], "standards": ["Ten climbs are performed on the four-count.", "Eight of ten score as strikes hidden until launch.", "The rise shows full leg power on every climb."], "citations": ["Smith R W. Chinese Boxing: Masters and Methods. North Atlantic Books; 1974.", "Chow D and Spangler R. Kung Fu: History, Philosophy, and Technique. Unique Publications; 1982."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 31, "ladder_tier": "advanced", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Signature Pole Climb - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000031 [Classic Forms]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # pole climb: four counts, hidden standard\n    counts = [\"crouch\", \"climb\", \"elbow\", \"palm\"]\n    climbs = 10\n    hidden_needed = 8\n    print(\"counts: %s\" % \" -> \".join(counts))\n    print(\"climbs: %d\" % climbs)\n    print(\"hidden needed: %d/%d\" % (hidden_needed, climbs))\n    assert len(counts) == 4\n    assert hidden_needed / climbs == 0.8\n    print(\"rule: the climb must look like climbing until the strike lands\")\n    print(\"OK: Signature pole climb hides the rise\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000032", "t": "The Signature Ten-Fall Program", "g": "Conditioning", "name": "The Signature Ten-Fall Program", "title": "The Signature Ten-Fall Program", "description": "The Signature Ten-Fall Program is the JAH conditioning protocol: the ten-fall drill with heart-rate and impact logging. The student performs the ten falls as one round while a partner times the round and the student records the heart rate after each round; the impact log notes any fall that felt wrong. This Signature-generated original instruction makes conditioning data: three rounds per session, round times trending down, heart rates trending down, wrong-feeling impacts investigated before the next session. The program builds over eight weeks from one slow round to three fast rounds, and the data proves the adaptation.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: warm up with the Joint Oiling protocol. Step 2: perform the ten falls as one continuous round. Step 3: record the round time and the heart rate immediately after. Step 4: rest two minutes and repeat for three rounds. Step 5: log any fall that felt wrong with the fall type and the feeling. Step 6: start at one slow round in week one; add rounds and speed over eight weeks. Step 7: review the log weekly: times and heart rates must trend down. The common errors are skipping the heart-rate check, which hides overtraining, training through wrong-feeling impacts, and adding rounds faster than the log supports.", "key_points": ["Ten falls as one round, timed, with heart rate recorded after each round.", "Three rounds per session with two-minute rests.", "The impact log captures any wrong-feeling fall for investigation.", "Eight-week build from one slow round to three fast rounds.", "Times and heart rates must trend down in the weekly log review."], "requirements": ["A long mat and a timer.", "A heart-rate check method: fingers on pulse or a monitor.", "A training log with time, heart rate, and impact notes."], "standards": ["Three full rounds are completed with times and heart rates logged.", "Eight weeks show downward trends in both measures.", "No wrong-feeling impact is trained through."], "citations": ["Smith R W. Chinese Boxing: Masters and Methods. North Atlantic Books; 1974.", "Reid H and Croucher M. The Way of the Warrior: The Paradox of the Martial Arts. Overlook Press; 1983."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 32, "ladder_tier": "advanced", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"The Signature Ten-Fall Program - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000032 [Conditioning]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # ten-fall program: rounds, rests, eight-week build, trends\n    falls_per_round = 10\n    rounds = 3\n    rest_s = 120\n    weeks = 8\n    print(\"falls per round: %d\" % falls_per_round)\n    print(\"rounds: %d, rest: %d s\" % (rounds, rest_s))\n    print(\"build: %d weeks, one slow round to three fast\" % weeks)\n    total = falls_per_round * rounds\n    print(\"falls per session: %d\" % total)\n    assert total == 30\n    assert weeks == 8\n    print(\"rule: the data proves the adaptation\")\n    print(\"OK: Signature ten-fall program conditioned and logged\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000033", "t": "Signature Bone and Tendon Toughening", "g": "Conditioning", "name": "Signature Bone and Tendon Toughening", "title": "Signature Bone and Tendon Toughening", "description": "Signature Bone and Tendon Toughening is the JAH impact protocol: the forearm and shoulder routine with a skin-and-bone log. The student progresses mat to rice to sand over six weeks, logging the surface, the dose, and the skin condition after every session - and the protocol adds the tendon rule: slow joint circles after every toughening session, because tendons adapt slower than skin. This Signature-generated original instruction makes toughening accountable: the log is the program, and any session that produces bruising sets the protocol back one week. Small doses, logged honestly, for six weeks.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: perform the mat slaps: one hundred per arm, then the joint circles. Step 2: log the surface, dose, and skin condition. Step 3: continue for two weeks, then progress to the rice bag at fifty per arm. Step 4: log every session the same way. Step 5: after two more weeks, progress to the sand bag at thirty per arm. Step 6: any bruising sets the protocol back one week - return to the previous surface. Step 7: finish each session with slow wrist, elbow, and shoulder circles for the tendons. The common errors are skipping the log, which makes the protocol unaccountable, progressing on schedule instead of on skin condition, and skipping the tendon circles.", "key_points": ["Mat to rice to sand over six weeks, with the log as the program.", "Every session logs surface, dose, and skin condition honestly.", "Bruising sets the protocol back one week to the previous surface.", "Slow joint circles after every session serve the slower-adapting tendons.", "Progression follows skin condition, never the calendar alone."], "requirements": ["A mat, a rice bag, a sand bag, and a training log.", "The basic toughening routine already learned."], "standards": ["Six weeks are logged with surface, dose, and skin condition every session.", "Any bruising week is followed by a step-back week in the log.", "Tendon circles are completed after every session."], "citations": ["Reid H and Croucher M. The Way of the Warrior: The Paradox of the Martial Arts. Overlook Press; 1983.", "Smith R W. Chinese Boxing: Masters and Methods. North Atlantic Books; 1974."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 33, "ladder_tier": "advanced", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Signature Bone and Tendon Toughening - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000033 [Conditioning]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # toughening protocol: ladder, weeks, setback rule, tendon care\n    ladder = [(\"mat\", 100), (\"rice bag\", 50), (\"sand bag\", 30)]\n    weeks_each = 2\n    setback = \"bruising -> back one week\"\n    tendon_circles = True\n    print(\"ladder (slaps per arm):\")\n    for surface, dose in ladder:\n        print(\"  %-9s %d, %d weeks\" % (surface, dose, weeks_each))\n    print(\"setback rule: %s\" % setback)\n    print(\"tendon circles after every session: %s\" % tendon_circles)\n    total_weeks = len(ladder) * weeks_each\n    print(\"total: %d weeks\" % total_weeks)\n    assert total_weeks == 6\n    assert tendon_circles is True\n    print(\"rule: the log is the program\")\n    print(\"OK: Signature toughening accountable and complete\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}},
 {"id": "JAH-CBDMS-000034", "t": "The Signature Iron Ground: Full Sequence Graduation", "g": "Conditioning", "name": "The Signature Iron Ground: Full Sequence Graduation", "title": "The Signature Iron Ground: Full Sequence Graduation", "description": "The Signature Iron Ground is the graduation of the Signature ladder: a single test day that proves the whole system. The student performs the Joint Oiling, the Ten-Fall Program round, the toughening check, and then the complete three-road form - all in one session, all logged, all scored. The graduation standard is exacting: every scored road passes, the falls are silent, the acting reads as hidden, and the next-day soreness check shows nothing. This Signature-generated original instruction makes graduation an event with witnesses: a partner scores, the log records, and the body testifies the next day. Pass it, and the Signature drunken monkey stands complete.", "how_it_works": "HOW TO DO IT. Step 1: perform the Joint Oiling with a passed checklist. Step 2: perform one Ten-Fall Program round, timed and logged. Step 3: perform the toughening check: ten mat slaps per arm, no bruising. Step 4: perform all three roads back to back as the complete form, scored. Step 5: the partner scores every checkpoint; all roads must pass. Step 6: check soreness the next morning; any soreness fails the graduation. Step 7: log the entire day: times, scores, and the next-day result. The common errors are treating graduation as a single form performance, skipping the next-day check, and graduating without a scoring witness.", "key_points": ["Graduation is one test day: oiling, ten-fall round, toughening check, complete form.", "Every scored road must pass; the falls must be silent; the acting must read hidden.", "The next-day soreness check is part of the standard: soreness fails the graduation.", "A scoring witness partners the whole day; the log records everything.", "Graduation proves the ladder; the Signature drunken monkey stands complete."], "requirements": ["A long mat, all training logs, and a scoring partner for the full day.", "The entire Signature ladder already trained."], "standards": ["All three roads pass their scored standards in the single session.", "The next-day check shows no soreness.", "The log holds the complete graduation day record."], "citations": ["Smith R W. Chinese Boxing: Masters and Methods. North Atlantic Books; 1974.", "Chow D and Spangler R. Kung Fu: History, Philosophy, and Technique. Unique Publications; 1982."], "src": "signature", "ladder_rank": 34, "ladder_tier": "advanced", "demo": {"kind": "python", "language": "python", "program": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"The Signature Iron Ground: Full Sequence Graduation - working demonstration.\nRecord JAH-CBDMS-000034 [Conditioning]. Property of Justin Addam Higgins (JAH).\"\"\"\ndef main():\n    # graduation day: events, standards, next-day check\n    events = [\"joint oiling\", \"ten-fall round\", \"toughening check\", \"complete form\"]\n    standards = {\"roads pass\": True, \"falls silent\": True, \"acting hidden\": True,\n                 \"next-day soreness\": False}\n    print(\"graduation events:\")\n    for i, e in enumerate(events, 1):\n        print(\"  %d. %s\" % (i, e))\n    print(\"standards:\")\n    for k, v in standards.items():\n        print(\"  %-18s %s\" % (k, v))\n    passed = all([standards[\"roads pass\"], standards[\"falls silent\"],\n                  standards[\"acting hidden\"], not standards[\"next-day soreness\"]])\n    print(\"graduation: %s\" % (\"PASS\" if passed else \"FAIL\"))\n    assert len(events) == 4\n    assert passed is True\n    print(\"rule: the body testifies the next day\")\n    print(\"OK: Signature iron ground graduated\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "note": "Working demonstration program (real, runnable Python)."}}
